# Post-auth navigation has one decider and one executor

Multiple screens redirect the user after auth/entitlement changes. Keep this split
or you reintroduce double-navigation flicker and "winning redirect" races.

- **Decide** the destination ONLY via `lib/navigateAfterAuth.ts`
  (`getPostAuthRoute` / `resolvePostAuthNavigation`). Do not hand-roll route choices
  (gating on `isEmailVerified`, `onboardingComplete`, entitlement) in a screen.
- **Execute** the redirect ONLY via `navigatePostAuth(route)` from
  `lib/postAuthNavigator.ts`. It de-dupes identical targets fired within ~1s.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — competing imperative redirect, bypasses the de-dupe
const route = resolvePostAuthNavigation(user, rc, deepLink, consume);
if (route) router.replace(route);

// ✅ GOOD
const route = resolvePostAuthNavigation(user, rc, deepLink, consume);
if (route) navigatePostAuth(route);
```

- `index.tsx` must keep its `isFocusedRef` gate so it never competes while an auth
  screen is on top. `EntitlementGate` stays declarative (`<Redirect>`); don't add an
  imperative redirect there.
- Plain `router.replace`/`router.push` is fine for NON-auth navigation (deep links,
  going back, sign-out to `/`).

## Paywall Back must never trap or force logout
- The paywall `Back` never signs the user out. Soft upsell (`?soft=1`) returns to the
  caller; the hard gate returns to `/` (welcome) and the user stays signed in. Explicit
  sign-out lives in the paywall footer.
- `index.tsx` must NOT passively redirect a gated (signed-in, non-entitled) user to the
  hard paywall — it renders the welcome "gated" state instead (Subscribe / Sign out).
  Use `isHardPaywallRoute` to detect this. Auto-redirecting here would ping-pong with
  the paywall's Back. Positive flows (verify, onboarding, entering when entitled) still
  redirect normally, and login/register/verify/onboarding still actively push to the paywall.
